---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Retry and failed-cone recovery — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-sky">For DevOps</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Retry and failed-cone recovery</h1>
            <p class="text-muted-foreground mt-2">
                Same authorization as Retry: DevOps, Project admin, Team-admin inherit, Platform admin. Developer is 403.
                Authors set in-run policy on
                <a href="/docs/developer-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">Step retry policies</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <h2>Two layers</h2>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">In-run retry</strong> stays on the same
                step execution: running → retry_waiting → queued, with backoff
                <code class="text-foreground">delay = backoff × multiplier^(attempt-1)</code>,
                capped at 900 seconds. Default <code class="text-foreground">retry_max_attempts = 1</code>
                means off until the published version raises it. Writes are default-unsafe
                for auto-retry; extracts and artifact-sql are default-safe. Unsafe blocks
                in-run auto-retry only. There is no auto-cone when in-run attempts exhaust
                — a human chooses full vs cone.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong class="text-foreground">Failed-cone</strong> is a new run that still
                <em>plans</em> the full graph, skips <em>execution</em> of source succeeded
                nodes, and rebinds artifacts. It is not a trigger-time subgraph and not
                “run from step X”. Full retry remains the UI/API default (<code class="text-foreground">mode=full</code>).
                Join mode does not change cone selection: a Wait any consumer that already
                succeeded is reused; parents that failed remain in the cone.
                <a href="/docs/developer-join-mode" class="text-link hover:underline">Wait all and Wait any</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>Failed-cone membership</h2>
            <p>
                Source run must be failed or abandoned, same project, same deployment,
                same published version, and the same resolved parameters. Cone is refused
                if those differ, or if DAG execution is off.
            </p>
            <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose">
                <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
                    <thead>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border text-left">
                            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Set</th>
                            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Members</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="text-muted-foreground">
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50">
                            <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-foreground">Seed (execute)</td>
                            <td class="py-2">Failed or abandoned on the source; also skipped or cancelled (never succeeded).</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border/50">
                            <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-foreground">Reuse</td>
                            <td class="py-2">Succeeded on the source. Status stays succeeded with outcome reused — not skipped.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium text-foreground">Expand</td>
                            <td class="py-2">A reused producer needed by a seed step whose artifact is missing, not available, expired, or has no checksum is moved back to seed.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                Independent succeeded trees stay in reuse. If every node is seed, the
                cone still records <code class="text-foreground">retry_mode=failed_cone</code>
                even though execution looks like a full rerun. Preview lists seed, reuse,
                expanded producers, and a warning when seed includes write steps.
            </p>

            <h2>Policy snapshot</h2>
            <p>
                Pipeline defaults, optional step override, copied onto step executions at
                plan create (same snapshot pattern as agent-pool assignment). Publish
                freezes version defaults. Dual-path: legacy serial uses an inner loop in
                the serial engine; cone requires DAG execution (and artifact/DuckDB flags
                when those handlers are in the cone). No extra feature flag.
            </p>

            <h2>Abandon</h2>
            <p>
                Grace first. Then retry_waiting only if attempts remain,
                retry_on_abandoned is true, and the step is not unsafe. Otherwise terminal
                abandoned.
            </p>

            <h2>Artifacts across runs</h2>
            <p>
                Cone clones artifact rows into the new run (new ids, same store URI and
                checksum, <code class="text-foreground">reused_from_artifact_id</code>).
                Resolvers never search other runs. Clones copy sensitivity and retention
                from the source. Audit payload has mode and counts, not URIs or secrets.
                Retry stays project-scoped.
            </p>

            <h2>Where to click</h2>
            <p>
                Preview the cone, then Retry / RetryRun, from run details or the schedule
                run list. Run History index and the visualizer do not expose the button.
                Missing project returns JSON 400 (not a login challenge) for the AJAX preview.
            </p>
            <p>
                Design-time fields, unsafe defaults, transient classification, and the
                three clocks (command timeout, lease/grace, cancel):
                <a href="/docs/developer-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">Step retry policies</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
